<MudText Typo="Typo.h3" GutterBottom="true">Blackjack Streak Simulator</MudText>
<MudText Typo="Typo.subtitle1" Class="mb-8">Does a "crazy" betting system actually change your odds? Let's find out.</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h5" GutterBottom="true">Where this came from</MudText>
<MudText Class="mb-8">
    Years ago, a friend and I went to Vegas and played blackjack together. He used a betting
    system that looked crazy to me at the time - but he walked away from that trip a winner, and
    I've wondered ever since whether that was the system, or just luck.
    <br /><br />
    It turns out the system has a name - <b>Paroli</b> - and mathematicians have already worked
    out its odds in exhaustive detail. I'm not a mathematician, but I am a software developer.
    So instead of doing the math myself, I built something that plays it out: thousands of
    simulated hands, with the results laid out for you to explore.
</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h5" GutterBottom="true">How Paroli betting works</MudText>
<MudText Class="mb-4">
    The idea is simple: bet more while you're winning, and reset the moment you lose. Say your
    base bet is $10 and you're chasing a streak of 3:
</MudText>
<MudSimpleTable Dense="true" Class="mb-4" Style="max-width: 500px;">
    <tbody>
        <tr><td>Hand 1</td><td>Bet $10, win</td><td>Streak of 1</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hand 2</td><td>Bet $20, win</td><td>Streak of 2</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hand 3</td><td>Bet $40, win</td><td>Streak of 3 - reset to $10</td></tr>
    </tbody>
</MudSimpleTable>
<MudText Class="mb-8">
    Lose at any point, and you're straight back to your $10 base bet. That's the whole system.
    <br /><br />
    Compare that to <b>flat betting</b> - what most players actually do - where the bet stays
    the same every single hand, win or lose. This app lets you run both side by side.
    <br /><br />
    Betting is only half the picture, though. Every player at the table - including the one
    being tracked - always plays the same well-established
    <MudLink Href="basic-strategy.html" Target="_blank">standard strategy</MudLink> for how to
    play each hand. Nobody's making judgment calls; only the bet sizing changes.
</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h5" GutterBottom="true">What you can do here</MudText>
<MudText Class="mb-8">
    Set a starting bankroll (say $1,000) and a goal (say $3,000), then run a batch of however
    many simulated attempts you like - 1,000 is a good starting point. The app runs them all and
    shows you what actually happened: how often the goal got hit, how often the bankroll went
    <i>bankrupt</i>, and how rough the ride was along the way.
    <br /><br />
    You can also step through a single simulated game one hand at a time. There's no real
    strategic reason to watch it live - every seat plays fixed basic strategy, nobody's making
    decisions - it's mostly here to show off Blazor's interactive rendering.
</MudText>

<MudText Typo="Typo.h5" GutterBottom="true">About this project</MudText>
<MudText Class="mb-8">
    This app is also a demo of my own skills - see the <MudLink Href="/techstack">Technology
    Stack</MudLink> page for a look at everything under the hood.
    <br /><br />
    I didn't write most of the code myself. I know these technologies and I'm an experienced
    developer, but for this project I worked closely with <b>Claude Code</b>, an AI coding
    assistant, discussing nearly every line of code so I understood exactly what was built and
    why. Every decision here was mine - the AI just helped me get there faster. Pairing real
    software engineering experience with an AI that can move fast turns out to be a genuinely
    effective combination.
</MudText>

